          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I am 68 with COPD and I have been more breathless for three days with green sputum. Is this an emergency, and what does guidance describe about exacerbations?",
          "answer": "**Please contact your GP or COPD team today, rather than waiting for a routine appointment.** A automated risk check rated your situation routine and found no emergency pattern, but that is not a clearance. You have COPD, your breathlessness is getting worse, and your phlegm has changed colour. The COPD guidance says worsening breathlessness that does not respond to your usual reliever needs urgent assessment. Another source says it needs prompt assessment.\n\n**Call emergency services now if any of these happen:** breathlessness at rest or difficulty speaking in full sentences; confusion or unusual drowsiness; blue lips or fingers; chest pain; coughing up blood; or a fever together with a marked change in your phlegm. The severe breathing difficulty guidance says drowsiness while struggling to breathe can mean carbon dioxide is building up, so do not wait to see if it passes. While you wait for help, sit upright, loosen tight clothing, keep the room calm, and use your reliever inhaler as your own action plan says. At 68, chest pain should also be checked for a heart cause first.\n\n**What the guidance says about exacerbations:** An exacerbation is an acute worsening of breathing symptoms beyond your normal day-to-day variation, usually triggered by a viral or bacterial infection. Each one tends to worsen quality of life and predicts future ones, and recovery can take weeks. Guidance recommends a written plan for managing exacerbations, a review after each one, and a check of your inhaler technique at that review. Other conditions that can cause similar breathlessness include chest infection, asthma, heart failure and flu. Signs of heart failure include ankle swelling, waking at night breathless, and weight gain over a few days.\n\n**What I could not find:** The retrieved material does not say whether green sputum reliably signals infection, or how strong the evidence for that is. It also does not describe any disagreement between authorities on managing COPD exacerbations; the only disagreement it contains concerns asthma, which does not apply to you. I cannot tell you whether this is an exacerbation, and I cannot advise on medicines. Ask your clinician to go through your personal plan with you.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
